372	// Why the PR-open POST must be status-checked, as a pure predicate: null when
373	// the PR was created, else the message to fail on.
374	//
375	// This is the one call whose silent failure is invisible AND self-perpetuating.
376	// `openMaintenancePull` finds only an OPEN PR, so a cycle that pushes its branch
377	// but fails to open the PR leaves nothing for the next cycle to dispose of, and
378	// the next — branches pile up nightly, the stamp never advances, and the run still reports `ok`
379	// because nothing read the status. The fleet ran exactly that way for two days:
380	// every consumer but the pilot sat on the same canon ref carrying 07-29 and
381	// 07-30 maintenance branches with no PR behind either.
382	//
383	// The usual cause is repo-level and invisible from here — "Allow GitHub Actions
384	// to create and approve pull requests" is off and the POST 403s — so the message
385	// names that remedy rather than leaving a bare status code.
386	export function pullCreateError(status, json) {
