393	// How an `auto-merge` member's PR actually lands, given whether this repo has any
394	// pull_request-triggered workflow at all.
395	//
396	// GitHub's auto-merge is a QUEUE FOR CHECKS. On a repo with no PR CI there is
397	// nothing to queue behind: the mutation is rejected ("Pull request is in clean
398	// status"), and since the failure was swallowed the PR sat open forever — the
399	// stamp never advanced, the next cycle reused the same PR, and a repo that asked
400	// for `auto-merge` got no merge at all. Seven of twelve consumers are in exactly
401	// that shape (no `pull_request` trigger anywhere in .github/workflows).
402	//
403	// So: no CI to wait for → MERGE IT, which is what `auto-merge` meant all along.
404	// CI present → arm, and let the checks gate it as designed.
405	//
406	// `hasPrCi` comes from ciDispatchPlan over the branch's own workflow files —
407	// `dispatch` (startable) plus `missing` (PR-triggered but not dispatchable). Both
408	// empty means no workflow anywhere runs on a pull_request, so no check will ever
409	// appear. That is a fact about the tree, not a race against checks registering.
410	//
411	// `gate` is what the BASE BRANCH requires (classifyMergeGate) — the thing
412	// GitHub's auto-merge actually queues behind. CI existing was the wrong
413	// predicate (#677): on an unprotected base the PR is mergeable the whole time
414	// its checks run, so `enablePullRequestAutoMerge` is rejected "clean status"
415	// every cycle no matter how green — arming there is doomed, and the delivery
416	// goes straight to verify-then-land instead. `unknown` arms: a gate we could
417	// not see is never merged past.
418	export function deliveryAction({ delivery, hasPrCi, gate = 'unknown' }) {
419	  if (delivery !== 'auto-merge') return 'none';
420	  if (!hasPrCi) return 'merge';
421	  return gate === 'absent' ? 'land' : 'arm';
422	}
