447	// --- Landing a PR the arm could not land (#455/#205/#95) ---------------------
448	// Arming is best-effort and can fail PERMANENTLY, so "arm and move on" is not a
449	// delivery guarantee. Two shapes, both observed across the fleet, both leaving a
450	// green PR open forever:
451	//
452	//   1. GATED CI. The block above assumes GitHub SUPPRESSES the pull_request run
453	//      for a GITHUB_TOKEN push. On several members it does not — it CREATES the
454	//      run and parks it at conclusion `action_required`, pending a human
455	//      "Approve and run" that nobody is watching for. The run never executes, so
456	//      it never reports; `enablePullRequestAutoMerge` then refuses the PR with
457	//      "in unstable status (required checks are failing)". Meanwhile the
458	//      workflow_dispatch run deliver() started on the SAME head sha ran and
459	//      passed. So CI did pass; only the phantom gated run says otherwise.
460	//   2. AUTO-MERGE OFF. The repo never enabled Settings → General → "Allow
461	//      auto-merge", so the mutation is rejected outright no matter how green.
462	//
463	// Both are settled by the same evidence, read from the runs on the PR's own head
464	// sha a cycle later: if CI has CONCLUDED and nothing actually failed, the content
465	// on that sha is verified and `auto-merge` means merge it. A gated
466	// `action_required` run is not a failure — it is a run that never happened.
467	//
468	// Deliberately conservative about MERGING, because that bypasses the arm:
469	//   - Anything still queued/running → `wait`. Never merge mid-flight, and never
470	//     bin a cycle whose checks might still land it.
471	//   - Any real failure (failure / timed_out / cancelled / startup_failure), or no
472	//     successful run to stand on → `close`. NOT a merge, and equally not a reuse.
473	//
474	// `close` is the other half of the fix, and the more important one. Reusing the
475	// open PR — force-pushing each cycle's converge onto whatever state it had
476	// accumulated — is what made a single failed arm permanent: the PR outlived every
477	// cycle, so one bad night was inherited forever. Baselining is a DETERMINISTIC,
478	// IDEMPOTENT converge; the previous attempt carries no information this one needs.
479	// So a PR that did not land gets closed and re-cut from scratch, and no PR ever
480	// survives more than one cycle. Pile-up is prevented by closing the old one in the
481	// same step that mints the new, not by reusing it.
482	//
483	// A `review` member's PR is the owner's to act on, on their own clock — never
484	// merged, and never closed out from under them either.
485	export const REAL_FAILURES = ['failure', 'timed_out', 'cancelled', 'startup_failure'];
486	
487	export function pullDisposition({ delivery, runs }) {
488	  if (delivery !== 'auto-merge') return 'keep';
489	  const list = (runs ?? []).filter(Boolean);
490	  if (list.some((r) => r.status !== 'completed')) return 'wait';
491	  if (list.some((r) => REAL_FAILURES.includes(r.conclusion))) return 'close';
492	  if (!list.some((r) => r.conclusion === 'success')) return 'close';
493	  return 'merge';
494	}
495	
496	// Why the merge had to happen here rather than through the arm. The log line has
497	// to name which shape this was, or the repo misconfiguration behind it stays
498	// invisible — and it is a REPO setting, so only a human can retire it.
499	export function mergeReason(runs) {
500	  // The no-remedy shape names no setting (#677): on a base that requires
501	  // nothing, landing here IS the design, and sending an owner to fix something
502	  // already correct is worse than saying nothing.
503	  return (runs ?? []).some((r) => r?.conclusion === 'action_required')
504	    ? 'its pull_request run is parked at action_required (never ran) while the dispatched run passed'
505	    + ' — check Settings → Actions → General → workflow-approval requirements'
506	    : 'its dispatched checks concluded green and nothing on the base branch queues an auto-merge';
507	}
508	
509	// Why a PR was closed rather than merged — the same visibility argument, for the
510	// case that matters more: a member whose CI is genuinely red.
511	export function failureSummary(runs) {
512	  const failed = (runs ?? []).filter((r) => REAL_FAILURES.includes(r?.conclusion));
513	  if (failed.length) return `failing CI: ${failed.map((r) => `${r.name} ${r.conclusion}`).join(', ')}`;
514	  return 'no successful run on its head sha';
515	}
